//! Hand-built cases as Rust literals (docs/phases/phase-3.md item 3.5c).
//!
//! Every case is one public key, one message (its digest is computed by
//! `sha2` in the test), one signature and the expected verdict, with the
//! reason. They were built once, outside this repository, with Python's
//! big integers (the primes, the private exponents, the malformed
//! encoded messages and the e = 3 cube-root forgeries) and OpenSSL 3.6.2
//! (`genpkey`, `dgst -sign`): every case marked "(OpenSSL)" is a signature
//! OpenSSL made, and the builder asserted that it decrypts to the encoded
//! message the builder had constructed independently, so the valid cases
//! check this crate against OpenSSL, not against itself. The forged and
//! malformed signatures are `EM^d mod n` of a deliberately wrong encoded
//! message, so each is rejected only for the reason the case names.
//!
//! Hex strings are big-endian octets.

use clazel_pkcs1::spec::HashId;

/// One hand-built case.
pub struct HandBuilt {
    pub name: &'static str,
    pub n: &'static str,
    pub e: u64,
    pub hash: HashId,
    pub msg: &'static [u8],
    pub sig: &'static str,
    pub expect: bool,
    pub why: &'static str,
}

